      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a16.18.1',
          kind: "text",
          refs: ['g16.18.8', 'g16.18.13', 'g16.18.16'],
          lemma: "σὺ εἶ Πέτρος, καὶ ἐπὶ ταύτῃ τῇ πέτρᾳ",
          title: "Petros, petra — the pun that split the church",
          body: "The sentence is a pun and a christening at once: *you are **Πέτρος**, and on this **πέτρᾳ** I will build my church* — Rocky and rock, and in the Aramaic Jesus actually spoke there was no distinction at all (**kepha … kepha**; Paul still calls him Cephas). Simon is being given a new name in the old biblical pattern — Abram/Abraham, Jacob/Israel — a rename that assigns a role. What role, exactly, is the most contested question in Christian history: Rome heard Peter himself, and his successors, as the rock (the verse rings the dome of St Peter's in two-metre letters); the Reformers heard the rock as Peter's *confession* — the faith that just spoke — noting that five verses later the rock is a stumbling-stone and that Peter never behaves like a pope in Acts. The honest report is that the text underdetermines the institutions built on it; what it plainly does is bind the church's foundation to *this man in the act of this confession* — the two cannot be pried apart. Two more first-appearances deserve their weight. **Ἐκκλησία** — assembly — occurs here for the first time in any gospel (and only in Matthew): Jesus, at the pagan shrine, announcing a *counter-assembly* he will build himself. And **the gates of Hades will not overpower it** — with the reputed gate of the underworld visible from where they stood. Gates are not siege weapons; they are defences. The image is not the church holding out against hell's assault, but hell's gates failing to hold when the church advances — death's kingdom as the *besieged* party. Given what is announced three verses later, that direction of attack is the whole point.",
        },
      ],
